---

<BaseLayout
  title="TDMMA Workshop 2026 — CASSA"
  description="CASSA's workshop on Time-Domain and Multi-Messenger Astronomy (TDMMA), 7–11 September 2026 at Independent University, Bangladesh — five days of lectures, hands-on tutorials and panels on the changing sky: transients and surveys, gravitational waves, TESS exoplanet follow-up, and future facilities."
  current="/tdmma-2026"
>
  <AnimatedHero
    theme="merger"
    eyebrow="CASSA Workshop · 7–11 September 2026"
    title="Time-Domain & Multi-Messenger Astronomy"
    lede="Five days on the astronomy of a changing sky — the transient universe, and the messengers that carry it to us — at Independent University, Bangladesh."
  >
    <Fragment slot="meta">
      <span>Sept 7–11, 2026</span><span aria-hidden="true">·</span>
      <span>10:00 AM – 6:00 PM</span><span aria-hidden="true">·</span>
      <span>IUB, Dhaka</span><span aria-hidden="true">·</span>
      <span>Lectures · Tutorials · Panels</span>
    </Fragment>
  </AnimatedHero>

  <!-- Page submenu: sits directly under the hero, then pins beneath the site
       nav for the rest of the page. Active section tracked on scroll. -->
  <nav class="subnav" aria-label="On this page">
    <div class="wrap subnav__row">
      <a class="subnav__brand" href="#" aria-label="TDMMA 2026 — back to top">
        TDMMA<span>2026</span>
      </a>
      <div class="subnav__links">
        {[
          ["about", "About"],
          ["programme", "Programme"],
          ["venue", "Venue"],
          ["speakers", "Speakers"],
          ["organisers", "Organisers"],
        ].map(([id, label]) => (
          <a class="subnav__link" href={`#${id}`} data-sec={id}>{label}</a>
        ))}
      </div>
      <a class="subnav__cta" href="#apply" data-sec="apply">Apply</a>
    </div>
  </nav>

  <!-- ── Overview ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <!-- Sections alternate plain / elevated down the page: about · programme ·
       venue · speakers · organisers · apply. The programme keeps --elev so the
       calendar's sticky time rail can mask scrolling cells with the same
       background. -->
  <section class="section" id="about">
    <div class="wrap">
      <Reveal><p class="eyebrow">About the workshop</p></Reveal>
      <div class="about">
        <Reveal delay={70}>
          <div class="about__lede">
            <Prose>
              <h2>One sky, many messengers</h2>
              <p>
                Astronomy no longer watches a still sky. Surveys like ZTF and the
                upcoming Rubin&nbsp;LSST catch the universe changing night by
                night, while gravitational-wave detectors listen to it through
                an entirely different
                messenger. <strong>Time-Domain and Multi-Messenger Astronomy
                (TDMMA)</strong> is where those signals meet. The <strong>time
                domain is this workshop's centre of gravity</strong> — the
                changing sky, and everything we learn by watching it change —
                with the other messengers entering as the sky's most violent
                events demand them.
              </p>
              <p>
                The visiting lecturer for the week is
                <strong>Dr.&nbsp;Andrew&nbsp;L. Miller</strong> — assistant
                professor at the International Centre for Theoretical Physics
                Asia-Pacific (ICTP-AP, UCAS Beijing) and a member of the
                LIGO/Virgo collaboration — who comes to CASSA for the
                gravitational-wave portion of the programme. He is joined by
                <strong>Prof.&nbsp;M&nbsp;Arshad&nbsp;Momen</strong>, a
                theoretical physicist at IUB working on quantum field theory,
                gravitation and particle physics, who takes the programme's
                high-energy neutrino astrophysics session;
                <strong>Dr.&nbsp;Syed&nbsp;Ashraf&nbsp;Uddin</strong>, a
                supernova cosmologist who uses Type&nbsp;Ia supernovae as
                standard candles to probe dark energy and the Hubble tension,
                formerly of the Carnegie Supernova Project and a member of the
                Dark Energy Survey;
                <strong>Dr.&nbsp;Jewel&nbsp;Kumar&nbsp;Ghosh</strong>, a
                theoretical physicist at IUB working on string theory and
                gravity, who brings the theory of black holes and compact
                objects to the gravitational-wave sessions; and
                <strong>Dr.&nbsp;Khan&nbsp;Muhammad&nbsp;Bin&nbsp;Asad</strong>
                on the radio time domain — pulsars and fast radio bursts with
                MeerKAT and LOFAR. Session speakers and further facilitators
                will be announced before the workshop.
              </p>
              <p>
                The goal is capacity building: participants leave able to read a
                transient light curve, follow a gravitational-wave alert, and
                start research in time-domain astronomy and the multi-messenger
                science that grows out of it.
              </p>
            </Prose>
          </div>
        </Reveal>

        <Reveal delay={130}>
          <aside class="about__sci" aria-label="The four key sciences">
            <p class="about__sci-h">Four key sciences · four days</p>
            <ol class="sci">
              {SCIENCES.map((s) => (
                <li>
                  <span class="sci__day">{s.day}</span>
                  <span class="sci__t">{s.t}</span>
                  <p class="sci__d">{s.d}</p>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ol>
            <p class="sci__coda">
              The fifth day looks outward — Einstein Telescope, SKA,
              Rubin&nbsp;LSST, CTA and TART — and closes with participant talks
              and a panel.
            </p>
          </aside>
        </Reveal>
      </div>

      <div class="facts">
